## `parse_define_directive`

This function validates a `.definefilter` directive. It first checks for the required arguments:

- If `bti->paramc < 2`, it errors that the directive `"must have name argument"`.
- If `bti->argc < 1`, it errors that the directive `"must have filter parameter(s)"`.

It then looks up an existing rule by name. The key logic:

```
flg = ((rp = acl_filter_rule::find_byname(bti->rules_list, bti->paramv[1])) == nullptr) ? true : false;
```

If `remap_validate_filter_args` succeeds and a rule exists, and `flg` is true (new filter), it appends the rule to the list via `(*rpp = rp)->name(bti->paramv[1]);`. Finally it calls `rp->add_argv(bti->argc, bti->argv);` to store arguments.

## `remap_validate_filter_args` — no-IP / match_all_addresses / reset logic

Note: The `match_all_addresses` **default fallback** ("treat like `@src_ip=all`") is actually in `process_filter_opt`, not this function:

```
if (rule->src_ip_valid == 0 && rule->src_ip_cnt == 0) {
  src_ip_info_t *ipi       = &rule->src_ip_array[rule->src_ip_cnt];
  ipi->match_all_addresses = true;
  rule->src_ip_cnt++;
  rule->src_ip_valid = 1;
}
```

Within `remap_validate_filter_args`, for the `src_ip=` option, when the argument equals `"all"`, it sets `ipi->match_all_addresses = true;`. The **reset logic** for duplicate entries:

```
for (j = 0; j < rule->src_ip_cnt; j++) {
  if (rule->src_ip_array[j].start == ipi->start && rule->src_ip_array[j].end == ipi->end) {
    ipi->reset();
    ipi = nullptr;
    break; /* we have the same src_ip in the list */
  }
}
if (ipi) {
  rule->src_ip_cnt++;
  rule->src_ip_valid = 1;
}
```

The comment explains this handles when `"we have the same src_ip in the list"`. Similar reset patterns exist for `src_ip_category=` and `in_ip=` options.

